  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records establish the exact alloy's FCC/B2 lamellar structure and dislocation-based composite behavior, show that 600 °C annealing can cause decomposition and hardening in closely related Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni alloys, identify L12-containing HEAs as thermally responsive, and show that FCC/B2 interface character controls stress transfer and cracking. Thus they suggest investigating intermediate-temperature aging, ordering, and phase boundaries. However, none reports enhanced L12 ordering within the FCC domains of AlCoCrFeNi2.1, jagged boundary-tip stress concentrations, or the specific non-monotonic 600 °C embrittlement/750 °C recovery. Combining those elements would require a substantial new mechanistic idea, so the result is only directionally reconstructible."
